French president Emmanuel macron says he  thinks President Trump will back out of  the Iran nuclear deal in the next two  weeks McCrone kept a three-day state  visit with an address to a joint meeting  of Congress where he urged the u.s. not  to abandon this deal he offered a sharp  contrast to president Trump's  america-first policy saying the US needs  to engage other countries and avoid  isolationism and nationalism  Face the Nation moderator and CBS News  senior foreign affairs correspondent  Margaret Brennan was one of the few  reporters to meet with micron yesterday  she's with us now from Washington  Margaret good morning the French  president says he thinks that President  Trump will almost certainly trigger a  period period of middle tension in the  Middle East if he withdraws from the  Iran deal so what could happen if the  President does not recertify well this  is a deadline of May 12th and president  Matt Crone believes that President Trump  will withdraw that'll spike tension and  a lot of this depends reaction wise on  how Iran particularly their hardliners  respond remember many were already  skeptical of making any deal with  America and if the u.s. breaks its  commitment it could have consequences  including restarting Iran's nuclear  development president Trump warned he  would respond to that  there are a number of potential flash  points in the next month there are u.s.  troops in close proximity in Syria and  Iraq to Iranian forces there also four  Americans still behind bars in Iran  whose fate may be in jeopardy but the  big gamble here for president Trump is  that using this tension and pressure  could lead to a new kind of diplomatic  breakthrough and it's one that the  French president says he wants to lead  of course the Europeans are worried that  if the president's gambling he's kind of  gambling with their money too so Angela  Merkel is coming from Germany next week  is she part of a coordinated effort here  to try to keep the president from going  this way on Iran absolutely she is you  know the leader of Germany it is the  engine of Europe but unfortunately she  doesn't have that warm rapport with  President Trump that the French  president does they've made clear  neither Merkel nor Trump really care for  each other here but she is someone to be  reckoned with in terms of leadership  in Europe she's going to argue a deal is  a deal America must abide by its  commitment here and trying to rewrite  rules we'll throw into jeopardy all the  other commitments that America has made  but if anyone believes that they can  succeed with President Trump in  persuading both Iran and the u.s. choose  cool tensions and engage in a new round  of diplomacy it is more likely to be  France as macron Germany's miracle of  course is key to any kind of  communication with Russia's Vladimir  Putin and very quickly Margaret there  was a difference between Matt crawling  in public with the president and then  what he said in front of Congress what's  the balance in terms of their  relationship after this trip well there  was some criticism wrapped in this  veneer of flattery from the French  president the address he gave to  Congress was very well received and he  championed a lot of traditional American  values he talked about the u.s.  upholding its commitments to NATO to the  UN the global order that America helped  build after World War two all those  things that sometimes seem at odds with  trumpism so it's not clear if that warm  relationship will translate into  changing the president's mind on any of  the issues on which they disagree all  right Margaret thanks we'll look forward  to Face the Nation Sunday morning as we  always do right here on CBS
 

